  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            Spanish voiced stop consonants (/b/, /d/, /g/) have two variants: a full stop and an approximant. \nThe /d/ sound can appear as a dental stop [d] or a voiced dental approximant [ð], especially between vowels. \nThe /b/ sound may be realised as a bilabial stop [b] at the start of a word or phrase, or as an approximant [β] between vowels, in which the lips approach but do not fully touch. \nLikewise, /g/ can be a velar stop [g] or a velar approximant [γ], particularly after vowels. \nUnderstanding these variants is important for accurate pronunciation and listening comprehension.\n"
    }
  ],
